now of a non-fraudulent misrepresentation or mistake he does not within a reasonable time manifest to the other party his intention to avoid it. The power of a party to avoid a contract for non- fraudulent misrepresentation or mistake is also lost if the contract has been so far performed or the circumstances have otherwise so changed that avoidance would be inequitable and if damages will be adequate compensation. (3) In determining what is a reasonable time, the following circum - stances are significant: (a) the extent to which the delay enabled or might have enabled the party with the power of avoidance to speculate at the other party’s risk; § 379 RESTATEMENT OF CONTRACTS—SECOND"